From 8c6175c2fbf238386c358059bc642d81a5a39adc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rolando Trevino <rolotrealanis@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 16:39:27 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] asahi: drive the M5 Pro and M5 Max through the Gallium M5
 path

The Gallium M5 (HAL300, AGX3c) path tests for G17G by name at every
site: tile programs, the Ioto graphics and compute program generators,
Gen10 descriptors, Gen5 samplers, the HAL300 initial PPP state, the
compact VDM pointers, the M5 ZLS pixel packing, the per-attachment and
per-layer splits and the CPU fallbacks. The device layer already admits
G17S (M5 Pro, T6050) and G17C (M5 Max) under ASAHI_M5_BRINGUP=1, and
lays out their VM as M5, but the Gallium driver then fell through every
one of those tests to the AGX2 encoders, so the background, end-of-tile
and draw state it built for them was pre-M5 state the firmware cannot
run.

G17G, G17S and G17C share the AGX3c instruction encoding, the HAL300
command stream, Gen10 descriptors and Gen5 samplers
(agx_chip_isa_encoding, agx_chip_command_stream_generation,
agx_chip_descriptor_generation, agx_chip_sampler_generation), and the
Vulkan driver takes every one of these encoders for all three through
agx_chip_g17_m5(). Ask that question here as well, so that the M5 Pro
and M5 Max get the M5 encodings and the same conservative CPU paths
(layer and attachment splits, CPU geometry, transform feedback and
mipmaps) that G17G uses.

Two shared helpers had the same G17G-only test and only Gallium calls
them on M5: agx_zls_pixels() now packs the 16-bit M5 pair for every M5
part, as hk_drm_m5_zls_pixels() does for Honeykrisp, and
agx3_g17p_fragment_blend_constant_base() admits every M5 part.

Timer queries, which G17G's M5 runtime cannot back, are advertised on
G17S and G17C: the G17 runtime maps timestamp objects and stamps M5
renders and dispatches, and desktop GL 3.3 requires them
(ARB_timer_query); without them the context stops at GL 3.2.

Kept on G17G only: the one-dispatch-per-submission compute split, which
answers the M5 kernel runtime's single-command bring-up submit path (the
G17 runtime that runs the M5 Pro takes mixed command arrays and orders
them with barriers), and the compiler's G17G hardware workarounds, which
the Vulkan driver does not apply on G17S either.

Offline, on the noop shim's G17S identity, the first fragment shader of
a GLES 3.1 smoke test could not even be compiled before this change (the
AGX3c packer refuses the constant program a preamble needs, and the
monolithic no-preamble compile was G17G-only); after it, every scene
compiles and submits.

G17G behaviour is unchanged. Rendering on G17S and G17C additionally
needs the per-render program storage list their kernel requires; that
follows.

Signed-off-by: Rolando Trevino <rolotrealanis@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: LLM Claude
